WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.390
Film Courage: So I know you had said in another
interview that Richard Avedon was one of your

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.559
idols [along with maybe] Francesco Scavullo,
all of these fashion photographers.

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:14.350
So it seems like that was your aim at that
time, either that or products?

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:23.270
Jay Silverman: Well, when I was going to photography
school you pick mentors if you are fortunate

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.770
enough to be motivated by them.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:33.480
In my case I had Richard Avedon, I had Pete
Turner, and I had Scavullo, Annie Leibovitz.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.040
You know these are people that I looked at
as someone I’d like to be like.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:46.640
And when I finished school I moved to New
York and it became almost a surprise to me

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.581
when I realized that “I think I can make
a living in Los Angeles.

00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:52.630
I don’t think I have to live in New York.”

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:58.850
And I knew back then that lifestyle was more
important to me than money.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:06.459
I did not want to leave my family and my friends
so I came back to LA and set up a business

00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:07.459
that way.

00:01:07.459 --> 00:01:12.270
Film Courage: Especially the allure of doing
a VOGUE cover, especially when you are just

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.270
starting out.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:19.060
Jay Silverman: Well it’s curious that you
say that because as a photographer at the

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.529
beginning of my creative career, I did everything.

00:01:22.529 --> 00:01:27.880
I did fashion, I did product shots, everything
but a wedding.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.219
And then you wake up one morning and somebody
gives you some advice, you know?

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:34.709
And that advice came at a high price.

00:01:34.709 --> 00:01:39.639
I paid a lot of money for someone to give
me advice when I was in my 20’s on how to

00:01:39.639 --> 00:01:40.880
go to the next level.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.880
And the advice came with a question.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:50.969
The question was very simplistically what
makes you different from everybody else and

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:56.420
he really screwed with me because he did not
want to tell me what the simplistic answer

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:57.759
was.

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:01.029
So I tried everything “Oh, I do great people
shots.

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:03.159
I build great sets.

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:05.539
I can motivate children.”

00:02:05.539 --> 00:02:07.259
And then he said “No…no…no.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.599
Think about it and get back to me next week.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:10.899
And I was terrified.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:12.980
I thought ‘how can I be so stupid?

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.150
He’s asking me a question about my own career?’

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:21.590
And then finally I spit out….“I’m a
problem solver!”

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.310
And the guy looks at me and I look at him
and says “Finally!”

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:31.280
And that really kind of encompasses everything
about creativity.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.230
How do you make a documentary?

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.030
You’ve got to solve the problem.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:42.290
How do you…in my case I came across a great
idea for a TV show called The Cleaner (based

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.890
on personal experiences) and how do you sell
it?

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.180
Well you wake up one morning and you sell
it.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.469
You know, you’ve got to solve the problem.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:56.930
In my case since I was half filmmaker/half
photographer I started videotaping the highlights

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.939
(the real highlights) that the show is about.

00:02:59.939 --> 00:03:05.469
It was the first AMC drama that they made
(it was way before The Walking Dead and all

00:03:05.469 --> 00:03:06.940
this other stuff).

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:11.499
And we were lucky enough to have Benjamin
Bratt as the star.

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:17.219
The character that he played on the TV show
was a gentleman that I knew in real life.

00:03:17.219 --> 00:03:23.359
So I filmed him multiple times talking about
what he did for a living and condensed it

00:03:23.359 --> 00:03:28.579
into a 60-second piece and that’s how I
sold the TV show.

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:34.749
And it wasn’t until 6 or 8 months later
that I’m working with a crew on a pilot

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:39.060
where they say “Do you know that this is
like one-in-a-million that you can sell a

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.469
show with no show of success?”

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:49.580
And that’s kind of how I’ve run my whole
career is trying to focus on my goal, what

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:55.279
is the real net choice that I’m going to
make.

00:03:55.279 --> 00:03:59.879
Am I going to make a movie about the circus
or am I going to go out and make a movie about

00:03:59.879 --> 00:04:03.040
somebody who goes out and saves people from
themselves.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:11.790
And that’s kind of why I have a portfolio
visually of different styles.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.620
I’m always interested in trying to grow.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:20.239
Film Courage: Well speaking of growing…looking
back on that Mr. Miyagi or whoever he was

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:24.280
who was sort of mentoring you, do you ever
think about what would have happened if you

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.950
hadn’t met this person (as frustrating as
that experience was for a little bit)?

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:35.280
Jay Silverman: Well, this person defined…it’s
funny you can hire a psychologist to come

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.889
into your business, they are called business
consultants.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.840
They come in, they try to read your mind,
they try to read what your goals are and they

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:48.919
try to help you understand how to get there
and the most basic thing is who are you?

00:04:48.919 --> 00:04:50.960
What do you really offer?

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.640
And it’s funny because as you listen to
other filmmakers talk, listen to other writers

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.910
talk, it all comes down to one thing and that
is defining what your goals are.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:11.340
And Bethany (who is my producer) brought me
the opportunity to go to a screenwriting fest

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:20.450
where producers and writers are able to meet
and actually get pitched.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:26.379
On one level it’s a little bit like one
of those real life dating situations where

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:31.270
you have 3 minutes to meet somebody and the
bell goes off and they switch.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:35.910
It’s a little bit like that but immediately
they are pitching you their movie.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:41.801
And I said to Bethany “I don’t want to
take any pitches for science-fiction, I don’t

00:05:41.801 --> 00:05:46.990
want to take any pitches for comedies, I don’t
want to take any pitches for…I want real

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:48.410
life stories.”

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:55.379
And it helped define my goal which is to find
something that I wanted to do rather than

00:05:55.379 --> 00:06:01.310
be inundated by these various other forms
and I’m not critical of them it’s just

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:01.860
not what I want to do?

